- Debt: Lower debt generally indicates better creditworthiness; high debt may lower credit score.  
- Overdue days: More overdue days indicate poor payment behavior, thus lower credit score.  
- Initial limit: Higher initial credit limit suggests higher trust by the bank, likely correlating with higher credit score.  
- BIRTHDATE: Age can affect credit scoring; middle-aged clients might have better credit due to stable income/history, very young or very old might score lower.  
- SEX: Gender generally does not directly influence credit score but could have some statistical correlation in the dataset.  
- EDU (Education): Higher education levels (University, College) often correlate with better income and credit scoring.  
- INCOME: Higher income generally correlates with higher creditworthiness and thus higher credit score.  
- TERM: Longer credit terms may be granted to more creditworthy clients, but very long terms might also indicate risk.  
- Credit history rating: This is a direct indicator of creditworthiness; better ratings (A1, A2, A3) imply higher credit scoring.  
- LV_AREA: Some regions may have better economic conditions, influencing credit scoring.  
- LV_SETTLEMENTNAME: Similar to LV_AREA but more granular; clients from major cities might have higher credit scores.  
- INDUSTRYNAME: Some industries may be more stable or higher paying, influencing credit score positively.  
- PDN (probability of default number): Lower PDN indicates higher credit scoring.  
- UNDERAGECHILDRENCOUNT: Having more dependents might reduce disposable income, potentially lowering credit score.  
- FAMILYSTATUS: Marital status may affect financial stability; married clients might have higher credit scoring.

